  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After a night of drinking and visiting a casino together, Chef Barinov and a very intoxicated Mikhail Dzhekovich arrive at the hotel so Mikhail can look up Nagiyev's home address.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Миша, не стоит он того.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Да ты ничего не понимаешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Я за Элеонорой полжизни бегал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы с тобой хорошо выпили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы даже в казино сходили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, уже поедем домой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет, это была только репетиция.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ты его адрес знаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет, у меня в отеле записано.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Давай заедем в наш отель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Спасибо тебе большое, Витя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты главное отдыхай и сил набирайся.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Миша, не стоит он того.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Misha, he is not worth it.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'Misha' and finishing on 'that'. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Да ты ничего не понимаешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You do not understand anything.' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'yes' and 'you' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Я за Элеонорой полжизни бегал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I chased after Eleonora for half my life.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'to run' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы с тобой хорошо выпили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You and I had a good drink together.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'we' and 'with' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы даже в казино сходили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We even went to the casino.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'we' before landing on 'to go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, уже поедем домой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe we should go home already?' The meaning hinges on 'maybe' working together with 'home' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет, это была только репетиция.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, that was only a rehearsal.' The '-a' ending on the past-tense verb for 'was' marks a female speaker or subject. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ты его адрес знаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you know his address?' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'you' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет, у меня в отеле записано.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I have it written down at the hotel.' In Russian, the idea of 'no' flows naturally into 'to write down' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Давай заедем в наш отель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's stop by our hotel.' In Russian, the idea of 'come on' flows naturally into 'hotel' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Спасибо тебе большое, Витя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you very much, Vitya.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'thank you', while 'proper name' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты главное отдыхай и сил набирайся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Just rest and gather your strength.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'you' and 'the main thing' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
